.Dd May 28, 2026
.Dt XTC_TLS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_tls_ctx_create ,
.Nm xtc_tls_ctx_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_tls_create ,
.Nm xtc_tls_create_transport ,
.Nm xtc_tls_ctx_set_sni_cb ,
.Nm xtc_tls_set_hostname ,
.Nm xtc_tls_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_tls_handshake ,
.Nm xtc_tls_read ,
.Nm xtc_tls_write ,
.Nm xtc_tls_shutdown ,
.Nm xtc_tls_wants_read ,
.Nm xtc_tls_wants_write ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_version ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_cipher ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_cipher_bits ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_alpn_selected ,
.Nm xtc_tls_has_peer_cert ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_verify_error ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_peer_subject_dn ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_peer_common_name ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_peer_issuer_dn ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_peer_serial ,
.Nm xtc_tls_get_server_cert_hash ,
.Nm xtc_tls_enable_ktls ,
.Nm xtc_tls_ktls_active ,
.Nm xtc_tls_sendfile
.Nd TLS over async sockets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_tls.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_tls
provides TLS 1.2 / 1.3 over xtc_io sockets.  The implementation
selects a backend at configure time
.Pq Sq --with-tls=openssl|none|auto ;
in v0.x only OpenSSL is implemented.
.Pp
.Sy Per-connection state machine :
.Bl -tag -width "wants_write" -offset indent
.It Vt xtc_tls_ctx_t
Per-process state: cert, key, CA bundle, version range, ALPN
profile.  Created once via
.Fn xtc_tls_ctx_create
with role
.Pq Dv XTC_TLS_SERVER or Dv XTC_TLS_CLIENT
plus opts.
.It Vt xtc_tls_t
Per-connection state.  Wraps a single fd.  Created via
.Fn xtc_tls_create "ctx" "fd" "&out" .
.El
.Pp
.Sy Non-blocking handshake :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_tls_create(ctx, fd, &tls);
for (;;) {
    int rc = xtc_tls_handshake(tls);
    if (rc == XTC_OK) break;
    if (rc != XTC_E_AGAIN) abort();
    uint32_t want = xtc_tls_wants_read(tls)
                  ? XTC_IO_READABLE : XTC_IO_WRITABLE;
    xtc_proc_wait_fd(fd, want, -1, &revents);
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tls_read
and
.Fn xtc_tls_write
follow the same pattern: return
.Dv XTC_OK
on progress,
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
when the fd needs more poll iterations.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tls_shutdown
performs the bidirectional close-notify dance; same WANT_*
translation.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_tls_handshake ,
.Fn xtc_tls_read ,
.Fn xtc_tls_write
and
.Fn xtc_tls_shutdown
return:
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_E_INTERNAL" -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_OK
Progress.  From
.Fn xtc_tls_read ,
.Va *out_n
== 0 is a clean EOF: the peer sent close_notify.
.It Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
Wait for the direction reported by
.Fn xtc_tls_wants_read
/
.Fn xtc_tls_wants_write
and call again.
.It Dv XTC_E_IO
The peer or the transport failed: the connection closed without
close_notify (a truncation), was reset, or a send/recv failed.  Tear
the connection down.
.It Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
Any other hard TLS error (a protocol violation, a failed certificate
verification, a non-TLS peer).  Tear the connection down.
.El
.Pp
An error is confined to the connection that produced it.  Every
backend clears its per-thread error state (OpenSSL's error queue,
wolfSSL's compat queue) before each of these calls and again after a
failure, so a bad peer on one connection cannot make a later call on
another connection served by the same thread fail.
This also holds in custom-transport mode when the callbacks yield the
fiber in the middle of a call (see
.Sx SNI AND CUSTOM TRANSPORT ) .
.Sh DEADLINES
The library applies no handshake or idle timeout of its own: the
caller drives every step, so it owns the clock.  Bound the handshake
with an absolute deadline and pass the time remaining to each wait;
the same shape bounds an idle read.  A peer that stalls mid-handshake
(slowloris) then costs at most the deadline:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int64_t deadline = xtc_clock_mono() + 5000000000LL;   /* 5 s */
for (;;) {
    int64_t left;
    uint32_t rev = 0;
    int rc = xtc_tls_handshake(tls);
    if (rc == XTC_OK) break;
    if (rc != XTC_E_AGAIN) goto drop;
    left = deadline - xtc_clock_mono();
    if (left <= 0) goto drop;                 /* handshake too slow */
    rc = xtc_proc_wait_fd(fd, xtc_tls_wants_read(tls)
                          ? XTC_IO_READABLE : XTC_IO_WRITABLE,
                          left, &rev);
    if (rc != XTC_OK) goto drop;   /* XTC_E_AGAIN = deadline hit */
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xtc_clock_mono
is virtualised under the simulator, so the deadline replays.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Vt xtc_tls_opts_t
fields:
.Bl -tag -width "verify_peer" -offset indent
.It Va cert_file , key_file
PEM paths for server-side certificate.
.It Va ca_file
PEM CA bundle for verifying the peer.
When
.Dv NULL ,
a verifying client uses the platform default trust store (OpenSSL
default verify paths, GnuTLS system trust, wolfSSL system CA certs when
built with
.Dv WOLFSSL_SYS_CA_CERTS ) .
mbedTLS has no platform store: a verifying client without
.Va ca_file
fails
.Fn xtc_tls_ctx_create
with
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL .
A server never trusts the platform store for client certificates.
.It Va verify_peer
Legacy: non-zero means
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_REQUIRE ;
0 means the role default.
.It Va verify_peer_mode
Tri-state peer verification, taking precedence over
.Va verify_peer
when not
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_DEFAULT :
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_NONE ,
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_REQUEST
(a server requests a client certificate, completes the handshake if
none is presented, and fails it if one is presented that does not
verify; a client treats it as REQUIRE), or
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_REQUIRE .
When both are unset the role default applies: a client verifies the
server
.Pq Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_REQUIRE ,
a server does not ask for a client certificate
.Pq Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_NONE .
.Pp
.Sy Behavior change :
Since 1.50 a CLIENT verifies the server by default; set
.Va verify_peer_mode
=
.Dv XTC_TLS_VERIFY_NONE
to opt out.
Before 1.50 a zeroed (or
.Dv NULL )
opts accepted any server certificate, and the GnuTLS, wolfSSL and
mbedTLS backends ignored
.Va verify_peer_mode .
.It Va alpn_protos
ALPN wire-form list: a length byte (1 to 255), then that many bytes,
repeated, up to the terminating NUL, as in
.Qq \ex02h2\ex08http/1.1 .
A list that is not exactly that (a zero length byte, or one that runs
past the end of the string) is
.Er XTC_E_INVAL
from
.Fn xtc_tls_ctx_create ,
on every backend and both roles.
.It Va min_version , max_version
.Dv XTC_TLS_VER_12
or
.Dv XTC_TLS_VER_13 .
.It Va cipher_list , ciphersuites_13 , groups
TLS 1.2 cipher list, TLS 1.3 ciphersuites, and key-exchange groups.
.It Va crl_file , crl_dir
CRL file / hashed directory; either enables full-chain revocation checking.
.It Va prefer_server_ciphers
Honor the server's cipher order over the client's.
.It Va passphrase_cb , passphrase_userdata
Supply the passphrase for an encrypted
.Va key_file
without an interactive prompt.
.El
.Pp
A server context is hardened by default (documented defaults, not knobs):
TLS renegotiation and compression are disabled, session tickets and the
session cache are off, and the moving-write-buffer mode required for
correct non-blocking sends is enabled.
.Sh SNI AND CUSTOM TRANSPORT
.Fn xtc_tls_ctx_set_sni_cb "ctx" "cb" "userdata"
installs a Server Name Indication selection callback on a
.Dv XTC_TLS_SERVER
context.  During the handshake, after the ClientHello is parsed, the
callback fires with the requested
.Va server_name
(or NULL if the client sent none) and may return a DIFFERENT,
pre-created server
.Vt xtc_tls_ctx_t
whose certificate, key, CA, and verify mode are used for the rest of
that handshake -- the multi-tenant / multi-host TLS case (mirrors
OpenSSL's
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_client_hello_cb
plus
.Fn SSL_set_SSL_CTX ) .
Returning NULL keeps the current context.  Registering on a client
context, or on a backend that cannot swap mid-handshake, returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tls_set_hostname "tls" "name"
is the client-side half: called before the handshake on a client
connection, it sends
.Va name
in the ClientHello SNI extension and enables RFC 6125 hostname
verification against the server certificate: a certificate that chains
to a trusted CA but is issued for a different host fails the handshake.
The name is enforced only when the context verifies peers.  A NULL or
empty name clears it (chain-only verification); it is a no-op on a
server connection.
BoringSSL cannot remove a name once set: there, clearing a connection
that already has one returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
and the old name stays enforced (use a fresh connection); clearing one
that never had a name succeeds.  Implemented on the OpenSSL, GnuTLS, wolfSSL and
mbedTLS backends; before 1.50 only OpenSSL checked the name and the
others returned
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS ,
which SChannel still does.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tls_create_transport "ctx" "transport" "&out"
wraps the TLS state machine around caller-supplied
.Va read_cb
and
.Va write_cb
callbacks instead of an fd, so the consumer owns the underlying
recv/send -- to feed bytes already read off the socket before deciding
to negotiate TLS (pushback), to weave the I/O into its own interrupt /
cancellation / fiber-yield discipline, or to handle a platform signal
window.  The callbacks are BIO-like: return the byte count, 0 for a
clean EOF, or
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
to signal would-block (the caller then drives its own readiness and
retries the TLS op).
A callback may yield the fiber (for example in
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd )
while other connections run on the same thread: the connection's
OpenSSL error-queue entries are saved before the callback and restored
after it, and anything another connection queued meanwhile is dropped,
so a failure on one connection is never reported on another.
A callback must not call
.Nm xtc_tls_*
on the connection it is serving.
These are OpenSSL-backend features; other
backends return
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Sh INTROSPECTION
Valid only after
.Fn xtc_tls_handshake
returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
.Fn xtc_tls_get_version
and
.Fn xtc_tls_get_cipher
return the negotiated protocol version and cipher name (or
.Dv NULL ) ;
.Fn xtc_tls_get_cipher_bits
the symmetric key strength;
.Fn xtc_tls_get_alpn_selected
the negotiated ALPN protocol.
.Fn xtc_tls_has_peer_cert
reports whether the peer presented a certificate that passed
verification;
.Fn xtc_tls_get_verify_error
reports the most recent peer-certificate verification result for
diagnostics: the backend verify code
.Pq OpenSSL Dv X509_V_* , Dv X509_V_OK No == 0 No on success
into
.Fa *x509_err
and a human-readable reason string into
.Fa buf ;
.Fn xtc_tls_get_peer_subject_dn ,
.Fn xtc_tls_get_peer_common_name ,
.Fn xtc_tls_get_peer_issuer_dn ,
and
.Fn xtc_tls_get_peer_serial
read the peer certificate (RFC 2253 DN, commonName, issuer DN, decimal
serial), rejecting an embedded NUL with
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL .
.Fn xtc_tls_get_server_cert_hash
returns the RFC 5929
.Sy tls-server-end-point
hash of the local certificate for SCRAM channel binding (MD5/SHA-1
signatures are hashed with SHA-256, otherwise the digest matching the
certificate's signature algorithm).
A backend that has not implemented these returns
.Dv NULL ,
.Dv 0 ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS .
.Sh KERNEL TLS
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tls_enable_ktls "xtc_tls_t *tls"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tls_ktls_active "const xtc_tls_t *tls"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tls_sendfile "xtc_tls_t *tls" "int fd" "int64_t off" "size_t len" "size_t *out_n"
.Pp
By default every TLS byte is encrypted or decrypted in user space and
copied between the caller's buffer, the backend's record buffer and the
socket.
.Fn xtc_tls_enable_ktls
opts one fd-mode connection into kernel TLS (Linux
.Pa tls.ko ,
FreeBSD KTLS): after the handshake the backend hands the session keys to
the socket, so
.Fn xtc_tls_write
and
.Fn xtc_tls_read
pass plaintext straight to and from the kernel, and
.Fn xtc_tls_sendfile
sends
.Fa len
bytes of file
.Fa fd
from offset
.Fa off
encrypted by the kernel without the file ever entering user space.
Call it after
.Fn xtc_tls_create
and before the first
.Fn xtc_tls_handshake .
It returns
.Dv XTC_OK
when the request is armed; whether the kernel takes over is decided at
the end of the handshake (module loaded, negotiated cipher: AES-GCM or
ChaCha20-Poly1305 on Linux) and reported by
.Fn xtc_tls_ktls_active
as
.Dv XTC_TLS_KTLS_TX
and/or
.Dv XTC_TLS_KTLS_RX
(0 = user space, or
.Fa tls
is
.Dv NULL ) .
A connection the kernel declines stays a fully working user-space TLS
connection.
.Fn xtc_tls_enable_ktls
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
when the backend or library build has no kernel TLS (only OpenSSL
implements it) or the connection uses a custom transport, and
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
when
.Fa tls
is
.Dv NULL
or the handshake has started.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tls_sendfile
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
with
.Fa *out_n
set to the bytes sent (possibly fewer than
.Fa len ,
like
.Fn xtc_tls_write ;
loop on it),
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
with
.Fn xtc_tls_wants_write
set when the socket is full,
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
when kernel TX is not active (fall back to reading the file and
.Fn xtc_tls_write ) ,
and
.Dv XTC_E_IO
or
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
like
.Fn xtc_tls_write .
.Pp
A kernel-TLS connection keeps fd mode's no-SIGPIPE guarantee: the
socket gets
.Dv SO_NOSIGPIPE
where the OS has it; elsewhere SIGPIPE is held off on the calling thread
for each TLS call, and a write the kernel accepted but could not deliver
because the peer has gone returns
.Dv XTC_E_IO
(the kernel itself reports the byte count).
Close the socket with
.Xr xtc_net_close 3 :
on Linux,
.Xr close 2
of a kernel-TLS socket whose peer has gone can raise SIGPIPE while the
kernel flushes a queued record, and
.Fn xtc_net_close
holds it off; a raw
.Xr close 2
does not.
